1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From: "Nancy High" <hitzel@att.net>
2 CONT Subject: Natan Veidel
2 CONT Date: May 18, 2018 at 3:16:23 PM PDT
2 CONT To: "'E Randol Schoenberg'" <randols@bslaw.net>
2 CONT Cc: "'Thomas High'" <Thomas.High@verizon.net>
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Randy,
2 CONT
2 CONT I was looking a little further at the Maor Katan family and got side-tracked onto Natan Veidel. When I saw that there was a Geni page on him, I thought I would send you what we have. It really is appalling that two people from two
2 CONC  different eras could be confused with each other, but since we have similar problems with our Esriels, I shouldn’t be critical.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Nathan Veidel had been in Worms, but it looks like he settled in Frankfurt at the time of his marriage sometime before 1547. He was used by the authorities as a translator and he had enough authority to place someone under a
2 CONC  bann (so I don’t know if he was officially the main Rabbi for awhile or whether in those days any Rabbi could issue a ban). In any case, these and other references to him are in Andernacht’s Regesten 1520-1616 Vol 2, items 850 an
2 CONC d 944. I can give you a full list if you want. His daughter, Rechlin, was married to Wenzel Auerbach Schwabach of the Scheuer, and they moved in January of 1563 to Merl bei Zell a beautiful little village near Cochem on the Mosel,
2 CONC  where the noble family was called “Hamm” – hence Ettlinger’s reference to “Merlin bei Zell in Hamm”. Wenzel is also mentioned several times in Andernacht.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our idea has always been that Wenzel and Reichlin had a daughter named Gutrat/Gertrut after his mother and that a marriage was arranged for her (probably by Wenzel’s relatives in Wien) to Samuel Feivel Lemle Theomim, and that she 
2 CONC was the mother of Natan Veidel Theomim. He was born around 1560, which is about when Rabbi Natan Veidel died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course, it would be really, really nice to have some concrete proof of this, but I mention it only because there seems to be so much in the old Vienna records which you might have access to. Hope springs eternal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shabbat Shalom!
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy
