1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rabbi Yissacher HaLevi Bermann (1661 - 1730 )
2 CONT
2 CONT also known as "Behrend Lehmann" the greatest of court jews in the 17th and 18th centuries.  His impact on Jewish scholarship in Germany is unquestionable, as his Klaus produced prominent leaders.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The World that was Ashkenaz" by Neil Rosenstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT BERMANN, ISSACHAR HA-LEVI: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philanthropist; born at Halberstadt Nisan 24, 1661; died there Tammuz 24, 1730; son of Judah Lehmann. At an early age he displayed great commercial enterprise. He afterward went to Hanover, and there became associated with the chi
2 CONC ef court agent Liepmann, who, appreciating Bermann's abilities and integrity, gave him his confidence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In this way Bermann had access to many princes, and several of them, such as those of Dessau, of Brunswick, and of Saxony, soon addressed themselves directly to him in their financial transactions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bermann was in especial favor with Friedrich Augustus II., elector of Saxony and later, king of Poland, to whom he rendered many services in the capacity of banker and as diplomatic agent in Poland. For these services he was rewar
2 CONC ded with the title of "Resident" of Poland and Saxony, by which title he is mentioned in the Polish chronicles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bermann used his prestige for the good of his coreligionists; and his intervention with the Polish lords saved many Jewish lives. The special protection that Halberstadt enjoyed during the reigns of Freidrich I. and Friedrich Wilh
2 CONC elm I. was due to Bermann's active influence. Generous by nature, it was his delight to foster Jewish learning. To this end he built a synagogue at Halberstadt, in which city many Jewish scholars found support, their works being p
2 CONC rinted at the sole expense of Bermann.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1696 Bermann obtained the permission of Friedrich Wilhelm to edit the Babylonian Talmud, copiesof which had become very scarce. The expense of this edition (Frankfort-on-the-Oder), amounting to $50,000, was defrayed entirely by
2 CONC  Bermann; and most of the 5,000 copies printed were presented to scholars.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lehman obtained in 1708 the permission of King August der Starke to settle down in Dresden in the forer Post House in the Pirnaischen Gasse together wit his brother in law Jonas Meyer from Hamburg, his eldest son Lehmann Berend an
2 CONC d their families and staff. Therefor he is the first Jew in Dresden after the exodus in the 16. century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Read more: http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=869&letter=B#ixzz168yLLsCE
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Issachar_Berend_Lehmann
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.manfredlehmann.com/news/news_detail.cgi/62/0
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Lehmann and namesake Behrend Lehmann
2 CONT
2 CONT Die Grabschriften des Alten Judenfriedhofes in Wien"
